or admission vs runtime errors — read server error-handling code. Both are \"read the EvalHub Server source\" investigations, same deliverable (an analysis of EvalHub Server status-reporting behavior). I'll combine into one Investigation epic with two concrete questions. That seems reasonable and matches \"don't split for the sake of splitting\".\n\nFor conditional branches: with one investigation having essentially a combined outcome space of 2×2 = 4 combinations... exceeding 3. Hmm. Alternatively, treat each question's impact separately:\n\nOption 1: One Investigation E001; downstream main-plan implementation epic E003 (all eval-hub-ui code) gated_by E001 with gate_failure_impact action=rewrite and fallback described. No branch files. This is simpler and the gate_failure_impact mechanism handles the uncertainty. The \"conditional branches\" for bounded outcomes is instructed, but when outcomes don't change epic existence/structure (just scope within one epic), branches may be overkill. Actually if outcome B for Q1, does the implementation epic still exist? Yes — the polling layer, state differentiation, elapsed time (via fallback), page visibility, error handling all still exist; only the benchmark-count display is dropped/changed. So the gate is `rewrite` (scope changes), not `remove`. Similarly for Q2.\n\nHmm, but the conditional decomposition section says \"If an Investigation epic has ≤3 bounded outcomes that change downstream structure, write one file per branch epic\". Does the outcome change downstream structure? Not really — same single implementation epic either way, just with/without benchmark progress display and with/without failure distinction. The structure (which epics exist) is unchanged. So I don't think branch files are needed; gate_failure_impact with rewrite suffices. \n\nHmm wait, but let me reconsider. If benchmarks[] is NOT populated mid-run, would we still build the detail-polling layer at all? The P0 HLR says detail polling for in-progress