gress display, state differentiation (Implementation)\"]\n  RHAISTRAT8005E001 --> RHAISTRAT8005E003\n  RHAISTRAT8005E002 --> RHAISTRAT8005E003\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementations) | The validation answers whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated mid-run and whether `message_code` distinguishes failure classes. The answers change what the implementation epic builds (benchmark-count progress vs. elapsed-time-only; pre-start failure distinction vs. single Failed state). True gate recorded on E003 as `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (producer of an artifact the consumer builds against) | The implementation consumes the UX specification (state icon/color specs, progress indicator pattern) as its design contract; the strategy marks UXD Support as required for exactly these deliverables. |\n| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 14 (independent inputs) | The UX specification is design authoring that does not need the API validation result first: the strategy already directs elapsed-time-primary presentation with benchmark count supplementary, so the spec is written to cover both possible validation outcomes. They start in parallel. |\n\n**Why no conditional branch epics:** both validation questions have bounded (≤2) outcomes, but no outcome changes downstream *structure* — the same single implementation epic exists either way, with scope adjusted (benchmark-count display dropped, elapsed-time fallback used; single Failed state fallback). This is exactly the `rewrite` gate captured in E003's `gate_failure_impact`, so no `-BRANCH-` files are warranted.\n\n**Why one implementation epic (no priority split):** Step 4's priority-split test fails for every P1/P2 HLR — benchmark progress, elapsed time, Page Visibility pause, and the pre-start failure distinction all share the same UI surface (the job table row), the same data structures (the detail-polling hook and job status state), and the same API endpoints as the P0 state-differentiation and auto-refresh work. They are tightly coupled to the same implementation surface and stay bundled.\n\n**Why no separate epic for the odh-dashboard BFF:** the BFF is a passive component (\"verify passthrough — no code change expected\") owned by the same team (ODH Dashboard) as the active component, so per Step 2 its validation is folded in as an acceptance criterion on the implementation epic.\n\n**Why no docs-authoring epic:** the strategy requires no substantive documentation content (new tutorials, doc topics, sample notebooks); Documentation Support is only a pending-review line item. Incidental doc touch-ups fall out of the code change and stay in the implementation epic's scope.\n\n**Why no tiered delivery:** no external dependency has uncertain timing — PatternFly and React Query both exist and are already in use (autorag/automl packages); all internal dependencies (plugin infrastructure, BFF proxy path, both EvalHub Server endpoints) are marked \"Exists\".\n\n## Component Mappings\n\nCanonical names taken from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n\n| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n|----|----|----|\n| eval-hub-ui (odh-dashboard plugin) | AI Core Dashboard | No canonical \"EvalHub UI\" component exists; the plugin lives in the odh-dashboard repository (Module Federation module `packages/eval-hub`) and is owned by the ODH Dashboard team, so the closest parent-level component is used. |\n| odh-dashboard (BFF) | AI Core Dashboard | Passive component (no code change expected); validation folded as an AC into E003. |\n| EvalHub Server (source of the open questions) | AI Evaluations | Closest canonical match for the eval-hub service (architecture context file `eval-hub.md` exists — component is in platform). |\n| Dashboard design system (UX spec) | UXD | Canonical component for UX design work; the epic produces design specifications rather than code. |\n\nBoth eval-hub and odh-dashboard have architecture-context files, so no new-component onboarding chain (Rule 20) applies. No repo/konflux/license onboarding is needed — the plugin is already onboarded per the strategy's prerequisites.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epics | Notes |\n|-----|----------|-------|-------|\n| HLR-1: Auto-refresh with list + detail polling for state transitions | P0 | E003 | Detail-polling portion gated by E001 |\n| HLR-2: Visual distinction of queued/running/failed/completed states | P0 | E002 (specification), E003 (implementation) | |\n| HLR-3: Non-blocking error handling during detail polling | P0 | E003 | |\n| HLR-4: Benchmark-level progress display with max-5 round-robin fetch | P1 | E003 | Gated by E001 (benchmarks[] mid-run availability) |\n| HLR-5: Elapsed time display from per-benchmark started_at | P1 | E003 | started_at source gated by E001; resource.created_at fallback exists |\n| HLR-6: Page Visibility API pause/resume | P1 | E003 | |\n| HLR-7: Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction via message_code | P2 | E003 | Gated by E001 (message_code semantics) |\n\nAll seven HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. Priority inheritance: E001 and E002 are prerequisites of E003, which carries P0 HLRs, so both inherit P0. An epic gating P0 work is implicitly P0.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Contingent P0 scope (non-blocking):** P0 HLR-1 embeds the detail-polling layer whose mid-run value depends on E001's outcome. If `status.benchmarks[]` is only populated after completion, P0 scope narrows to list-driven state transitions plus elapsed time from `resource.created_at`, and the strategy's benchmark-progress acceptance criterion cannot be met. Handled via the rewrite gate on E003; the fallback is already designed into the strategy (elapsed time as primary indicator).\n- **Pending human process gates:** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Documentation Support, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all \"_Pending human review_\" in the strategy. The implementation epic carries `human_process_gates = -1` accordingly.\n- **Proposed acceptance criteria:** the strategy's ACs are explicitly marked \"requires PM/Engineering validation\"; ACs were decomposed as written but may change on that validation.\n- **Deferred scalability (documented, not a trap):** scalability optimization for high concurrent user counts is deferred per Staff Engineer direction; load is bounded at max 5 concurrent detail requests per session, visible-page jobs only, with background-tab pause.\n- **Single-benchmark coarseness (risk, mitigated in-scope):** benchmark X/N progress is coarse for 1-benchmark jobs; the strategy's mitigation (elapsed time as primary indicator) is captured in the implementation epic's scope.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. No external dependency has uncertain timing (PatternFly, React Query, and all internal dependencies are marked \"Exists\"), so there is no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39698,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a85a41162facd59f","agent_id":"a6de3a8d673d618fd","session_id":"f780de70-496a-445e-b2ff-c0c509886095","uuid":"91582485-962d-4eee-895f-5b74bd192260","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:43:23.954Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8005"}
