{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-a0efaece-502d-4f7f-9c48-4e52738bea8c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b09a1a64e6550e27","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8005-kyg0mt8n/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8005-E001.md","content":"# Validate mid-run availability of benchmark progress and failure-code data in the EvalHub Server job status API\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the two open questions from the strategy that gate the EvalHub UI progress and state-visibility features. Both concern what the EvalHub Server actually returns while an evaluation job is still running:\n\n1. Is `status.benchmarks[]` in the `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}` detail response populated with per-benchmark status entries (per-benchmark `status`, `started_at`, `completed_at`) **while the job is running**, or only after completion? If only on completion, benchmark-level progress (\"Benchmarks: X/N complete\") and `started_at`-based elapsed time are unavailable mid-run, and the UI can only show top-level state plus elapsed time derived from `resource.created_at`.\n2. What values does `status.message.message_code` take for pre-start failures (admission errors, resource quota, scheduling failures) versus runtime failures? Are the two classes reliably distinguishable, or is a fallback to a single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text required?\n\nThe EvalHub Server is an open Go repository (architecture context: `eval-hub.md`), so both questions are answerable primarily by reading the server source code that constructs job status responses and emits error messages, supplemented by the published API reference. The server supports a local/development mode (identity-header enforcement bypassed, SQLite storage backend), though empirically confirming mid-run population for a live evaluation job would require a cluster, since evaluation workloads are dispatched as Kubernetes batch Jobs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Trace the EvalHub Server code paths that build the job status object for the detail endpoint: where `status.benchmarks[]` entries are created and updated relative to the job lifecycle (job admission, per-benchmark start, per-benchmark completion, job completion), and which fields each entry carries at each point.\n- Trace the error-reporting paths that populate `status.message` and `status.message.message_code`: enumerate the codes produced by admission/scheduling/quota failures versus runtime execution failures, and note any scenarios where the code is absent or ambiguous.\n- Cross-check findings against the published API reference for `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}` and `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs`.\n- Produce a findings report with a clear pass/fail conclusion per question, source citations, and — for each unfavorable outcome — confirmation that the strategy's fallback UI behavior is feasible against the actual API data (elapsed time from `resource.created_at`; single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text as supplementary detail).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A concrete, source-cited answer to whether `status.benchmarks[]` entries (with per-benchmark status, `started_at`, `completed_at`) are present in detail responses while a job is running, including the lifecycle point at which each entry appears.\n- An enumerated list of `status.message.message_code` values produced by pre-start failure paths (admission, quota, scheduling) versus runtime failure paths, with a conclusion on whether the two classes are reliably distinguishable across error scenarios.\n- For each unfavorable outcome, a confirmed statement of the fallback UI behavior and the exact fields it can rely on.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\nThis investigation does not implement any HLR directly; it gates the implementation of:\n\n- HLR-1 (P0) — the detail-polling portion of the auto-refresh mechanism\n- HLR-4 (P1) — benchmark-level progress display\n- HLR-5 (P1) — elapsed time display from per-benchmark `started_at` (fallback: `resource.created_at`)\n- HLR-7 (P2) — pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction via `message_code`\n\n## Open Questions Resolved\n\n- \"Is `status.benchmarks[]` populated with per-benchmark status entries while a job is still running, or only after completion?\" (strategy Open Questions table, owner: TrustyAI / EvalHub team)\n- \"What values does `status.message.message_code` take for pre-start failures vs. runtime failures?\" (strategy Open Questions table, owner: TrustyAI / EvalHub team)\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":45554,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a85a41162facd59f","agent_id":"a6de3a8d673d618fd","session_id":"f780de70-496a-445e-b2ff-c0c509886095","uuid":"8571729d-3284-4a60-b1d9-d77ff98eeaf0","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:43:39.566Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8005"}
