{"type":"tool_progress","tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a52dff944f0f30f8-heartbeat-0","tool_name":"Bash","parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a52dff944f0f30f8","elapsed_time_seconds":30,"heartbeat":true,"session_id":"f780de70-496a-445e-b2ff-c0c509886095","uuid":"308b2c54-1ab7-4d6d-844d-674a661b8551"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-92be1954-dd32-4374-9911-31e8cfdfe6bf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ac3ea41f342a01f","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8005-kyg0mt8n/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8005-E003.md","content":"# EvalHub UI two-layer polling, progress display, and state differentiation\n\n## Description\n\nImplement real-time evaluation job progress and state visibility on the EvalHub Evaluations landing page in the eval-hub-ui odh-dashboard plugin. The existing list endpoint (`GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs`, 30-second polling via `useEvaluationJobs` / `useFetchState`) continues to populate the job table with top-level state. A new detail-polling layer fetches `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}` for in-progress jobs on the currently displayed table page to obtain benchmark-level progress and per-benchmark timestamps, and the job table gains state differentiation, progress, and elapsed-time display per the UX specification. No EvalHub Server API changes are made — the feature works entirely within the data the two existing endpoints provide, per Staff Engineer direction.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Detail-polling layer (Layer 2)**: a polling hook that identifies jobs in a non-terminal state on the currently displayed table page (client-side pagination: default 20 rows, options 5/10/20 per `tablePaginationConstants.ts`) and fetches their detail endpoints on a 10-second cycle; when all detail fetches for the current page complete, a 10-second timer starts before the next cycle; changing the table page immediately retargets polling to the newly visible jobs; jobs not in view are not polled.\n- **Concurrency control**: maximum 5 concurrent detail requests, dispatched round-robin — as one request completes, the next pending visible in-progress job is fetched, keeping at most 5 in flight.\n- **Progress and elapsed time**: benchmark-level progress as \"Benchmarks: X/N complete\" (count of `status.benchmarks[]` entries with `status: \"completed\"`); elapsed time computed client-side from the earliest per-benchmark `started_at` (falling back to `resource.created_at` when no benchmark has started); until the first detail fetch returns for a job, only top-level state from the list endpoint is shown. Elapsed time is the primary progress indicator; benchmark count is supplementary.\n- **State visual differentiation**: distinct PatternFly icon, color, and label per state (Queued/Pending, Running, Failed, Completed) derived from the list endpoint's `status.state`, with `status.message` as error context; pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction via `status.message.message_code` where available (\"Failed (not started)\" vs. \"Failed\"), falling back to a single \"Failed\" state with message text when the code is ambiguous or absent — all per the UX specification.\n- **Retry-on-error**: React Query pattern per the autorag reference implementation (`usePipelineRunQuery`): `placeholderData: (previousData) => previousData` preserves the last successful response during transient errors, retry with exponential backoff (`RETRY_DELAY_MS * 2^attempt + jitter`, up to `MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`), 4xx treated as non-retryable, 5xx/network retried; on errors a non-blocking inline warning (PatternFly Alert variant \"warning\") indicates status updates are temporarily unavailable while the last successfully fetched data remains displayed; a failed detail request does not stop the round-robin.\n- **Page Visibility API**: polling pauses when the browser tab is backgrounded and resumes on focus, triggering a single polling cycle immediately on resume.\n- **Degradation**: if the detail endpoint is unavailable, rows degrade gracefully to top-level state from the list endpoint; the existing list polling and table rendering continue to function as-is.\n- **BFF verification**: verify the odh-dashboard BFF proxy transparently passes all detail-response fields through the existing `/eval-hub/api/*` proxy path (no code change expected — existing pattern for Module Federation plugins).\n- Incidental documentation touch-ups arising from this change (captions, references) are included in scope.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an evaluation job is running with 3 of 5 benchmarks completed, when the user views the Evaluations landing page and the detail-polling cycle has completed for that job, then benchmark-level progress is displayed (e.g., \"Benchmarks: 3/5 complete\"), measured by the count matching the entries with `status: \"completed\"` in the detail response. *(Contingent on the API data-availability validation; if mid-run population is absent, elapsed time from `resource.created_at` is the primary indicator and benchmark counts are omitted — see gate fallback.)*\n- Given an evaluation job transitions from running to complete, when the user is on the Evaluations landing page without manually refreshing, then the state badge updates within the list endpoint's 30-second polling interval, measured by the row's state label updating automatically.\n- Given a job has been running for 4 minutes, when the detail-polling cycle has fetched its detail endpoint, then elapsed time is displayed (e.g., \"Elapsed: 4m 12s\"), measured by the difference between current time and the earliest per-benchmark `started_at` (or `resource.created_at` fallback).\n- Given a queued/pending job, when the user views the landing page, then the row shows a distinct \"Queued\" treatment visually distinguishable from \"Running\" and \"Failed\", measured by distinct icon, color, and label per the UX specification.\n- Given a job failed before execution started (admission error indicated by `status.message.message_code`), when the user views the landing page, then the row displays a treatment distinct from a job that ran and then failed, measured by a label reflecting the failure type and a distinct icon/color. *(Subject to the message_code findings; fallback is a single \"Failed\" state with message text.)*\n- Given the EvalHub Server is temporarily unreachable during detail polling, when a 5xx or network error occurs on a detail fetch, then the last successfully fetched progress remains displayed, a non-blocking inline warning is shown, and the round-robin continues with the next in-progress job while the failed request retries with exponential backoff (up to 5 retries), measured by stale data still rendering and the warning Alert being visible.\n- Given the user navigates away from the browser tab, when the tab loses focus, then detail polling pauses with no further requests until focus returns, at which point a single cycle triggers immediately, measured by no detail-endpoint network requests while backgrounded (browser dev tools Network tab).\n- At most 5 concurrent detail requests are in flight per user session, only for in-progress jobs visible on the current table page, verified in testing.\n- The odh-dashboard BFF proxy passes all detail-response fields through unmodified (verified against a live detail response).\n- The implementation follows the agreed UX specification for state icons/colors/labels and the progress indicator pattern.\n- CI/build pipeline for the odh-dashboard repository is green with the change.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): auto-refresh mechanism using existing list polling for top-level state plus the new detail polling for in-progress job progress\n- HLR-2 (P0): visual distinction between queued/pending, running, failed, and completed states\n- HLR-3 (P0): non-blocking error handling during detail polling (last state preserved, inline warning, exponential backoff per the autorag pattern)\n- HLR-4 (P1): benchmark-level progress display with max-5 concurrent round-robin fetch for in-progress jobs on the current table page\n- HLR-5 (P1): elapsed time display computed client-side from per-benchmark `started_at`\n- HLR-6 (P1): polling pauses when the tab is backgrounded and resumes immediately on focus\n- HLR-7 (P2): pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction using `status.message.message_code` where available\n\n## Gate Dependency\n\nThis epic is gated by the EvalHub Server API data-availability validation (whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated mid-run and whether `message_code` distinguishes failure classes). If the validation resolves unfavorably, the scope is rewritten rather than removed: benchmark-count progress is dropped in favor of elapsed time from `resource.created_at` as the primary indicator, and/or a single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text replaces the pre-start/runtime distinction. The design work it builds against is the progress indicator and state differentiation UX specification.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":47319,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a85a41162facd59f","agent_id":"a6de3a8d673d618fd","session_id":"f780de70-496a-445e-b2ff-c0c509886095","uuid":"bfcfa76c-b1c5-4a49-9d0a-bbeb654add6c","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:44:17.070Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8005"}
