{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b06588fa89c7c2e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\ttitle: 'EvalHub UI: Real-Time Evaluation Job Progress and State Visibility'\n4\tstatus: Release Pending\n5\tpriority: Major\n6\tlabels:\n7\t- dashboard-purple-scrum\n8\t- epic-rollout\n9\t- refined\n10\t- ux-debt\n11\tlinks:\n12\t- 'source-rfe: RHAIRFE-7236'\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## Business Need (from RFE)\n16\t\n17\t> The full business need is maintained in the source RFE: RHAIRFE-7236\n18\t\n19\t## Strategy (AI Generated by Agentic SDLC Pipeline)\n20\t\n21\t### TL;DR\n22\t\n23\tAdd real-time evaluation job progress indicators, elapsed time display, and auto-refreshing state transitions to the EvalHub Evaluations landing page in the RHOAI dashboard. The existing list endpoint (`GET /evaluations/jobs`) provides top-level state for the job table with its current 30s polling; a new detail-polling layer fetches `GET /evaluations/jobs/{id}` for in-progress jobs on the visible table page to obtain benchmark-level progress (e.g., \"Benchmarks: 2/5 complete\") and per-benchmark timestamps for elapsed time. Detail polling runs at 10s intervals with a max-5 concurrent request cap in round-robin, using the autorag React Query retry-with-backoff pattern. Polling pauses when the browser tab is backgrounded (Page Visibility API).\n24\t\n25\t**Components**: eval-hub-ui (odh-dashboard plugin), odh-dashboard (BFF proxy)\n26\t\n27\t### Technical Approach\n28\t\n29\tThe Evaluations landing page currently fetches all jobs via a single `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs` call (in `useEvaluationJobs` / `k8s.ts`) with 30-second polling via `useFetchState`. This list endpoint returns `status.state` and `status.message` but omits `status.benchmarks[]` -- the per-benchmark progress data (with `started_at`, `completed_at`, and per-benchmark `status`) is only available from the detail endpoint `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}`. Per Staff Engineer direction, the strategy assumes no additional data beyond what these two existing API endpoints currently provide.\n30\t\n31\t**Two-Layer Polling Architecture**: The strategy introduces a two-layer polling model. Layer 1 (existing): the list endpoint continues polling at its current 30-second interval to populate the job table with top-level state transitions (pending, running, completed, failed). Layer 2 (new): a detail-polling hook identifies which jobs on the currently displayed table page are in a non-terminal state and fetches their detail endpoints to obtain `status.benchmarks[]` for progress display. Layer 2 runs on a 10-second cycle. When all detail fetches for the current page complete, a 10-second timer starts before the next cycle. Only jobs visible on the current table page are polled -- changing the page (client-side pagination: default 20 rows, options 5/10/20 per `tablePaginationConstants.ts`) immediately updates which jobs are targeted by detail polling.\n32\t\n33\t**Concurrency Control**: Detail polling is capped at 5 concurrent requests. If the current page has more than 5 in-progress jobs, requests are dispatched in round-robin: as one request completes, the next pending job is fetched, keeping exactly 5 in flight until all in-progress jobs on the page have been updated. This limits Server load to at most 5 parallel detail requests per user session regardless of how many jobs are running.\n34\t\n35\t**Progress Model**: For a job with N benchmarks, progress is expressed as \"Benchmarks: X/N complete\" where X is the count of entries in `status.benchmarks[]` with `status: \"completed\"`. Elapsed time is computed client-side as the difference between the current time and the earliest `started_at` timestamp across all benchmarks (or `resource.created_at` if no benchmark has started). Where a benchmark has both `started_at` and `completed_at`, per-benchmark duration is also available. Until the first detail-fetch returns for a given job, the UI shows only the top-level state from the list endpoint (no benchmark progress or elapsed time). This benchmark-level granularity is coarser than task-level progress but directly reflects what the Server reports without invention.\n36\t\n37\t**Retry-on-Error**: Detail polling adopts the React Query pattern established by the autorag package (`usePipelineRunQuery`): `placeholderData: (previousData) => previousData` preserves the last successful response during transient errors, `retry` with exponential backoff (`RETRY_DELAY_MS * 2^attempt + jitter`, up to `MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`), 4xx errors treated as non-retryable, 5xx/network errors retried. When polling encounters errors, the UI displays a non-blocking inline warning (PatternFly Alert variant \"warning\") indicating that status updates are temporarily unavailable, while continuing to show the last successfully fetched data. If a single detail request fails, the round-robin continues with the next job -- partial progress data is acceptable.\n38\t\n39\t**Page Visibility API**: Polling pauses when the browser tab is not in the foreground and resumes when the tab regains focus. This reduces Server load from inactive tabs. On resume, a single polling cycle is triggered immediately so the UI refreshes without waiting for the next interval.\n40\t\n41\t**State Visual Differentiation**: The job list distinguishes states based on the `status.state` field from the list endpoint (available for all jobs without detail fetch). The mapping: (1) Queued/Pending -- `status.state` is `pending`, (2) Running -- `status.state` indicates active execution, (3) Failed -- `status.state` indicates failure, with `status.message` providing error context, (4) Completed -- `status.state` is `completed`. Each state uses a distinct PatternFly status icon, color, and label. The distinction between pre-start failure and runtime failure uses `status.message.message_code` where available -- if the message indicates an admission error, scheduling failure, or resource constraint, the UI renders \"Failed (not started)\" vs. \"Failed\" for runtime errors. This approach works within the existing API without requiring new state enum values.\n42\t\n43\t**Authentication Flow**: All BFF requests flow through the existing auth chain: kube-rbac-proxy sidecar (port 8443) enforces OpenShift RBAC, then the dashboard backend (port 8080) proxies to the eval-hub-ui container (port 8543), which forwards the user's access token via `x-forwarded-access-token` header to the EvalHub Server for TokenReview and SubjectAccessReview. No new auth paths are introduced. Multi-tenant isolation is preserved via the `X-Tenant` header namespace scoping.\n44\t\n45\t### Affected Components\n46\t\n47\t| Component | Change | Owner Team |\n48\t| --- | --- | --- |\n49\t| eval-hub-ui (odh-dashboard plugin) | Add detail-polling layer with round-robin concurrency control, benchmark-level progress display, state differentiation UI, elapsed time computation, Page Visibility API integration, error warning banner | ODH Dashboard / EvalHub UI |\n50\t| odh-dashboard (BFF) | Verify BFF proxy passes through all response fields from eval-hub-ui plugin (existing behavior -- no code change expected) | ODH Dashboard |\n51\t\n52\t### Impacted Teams\n53\t\n54\t| Team | Components Owned | Involvement |\n55\t| --- | --- | --- |\n56\t| ODH Dashboard | odh-dashboard, eval-hub-ui plugin | Primary implementation: frontend polling, progress UI, state differentiation, error handling |\n57\t| UX/Design | Dashboard design system | Progress indicator pattern review, state icon/color specifications, PatternFly alignment |\n58\t\n59\t### High Level Requirements\n60\t\n61\t* \\[P0\\] Auto-refresh mechanism for the Evaluations landing page job list showing state transitions (pending -> running -> complete/failed) without manual page refresh, using the existing list endpoint polling for top-level state and new detail endpoint polling for in-progress job progress\n62\t* \\[P0\\] Visual distinction between queued/pending, actively running, failed, and completed states with distinct labels, icons, and color treatments derived from the list endpoint's `status.state` field\n63\t* \\[P0\\] Non-blocking error handling during detail polling: last successful state preserved, inline warning displayed, retry with exponential backoff per the autorag pattern\n64\t* \\[P1\\] Benchmark-level progress display for running jobs (e.g., \"Benchmarks: 2/5 complete\") derived from the detail endpoint's `status.benchmarks[]` array, with a max-5 concurrent round-robin fetch for in-progress jobs on the current table page\n65\t* \\[P1\\] Elapsed time display for running jobs, computed client-side from per-benchmark `started_at` timestamps obtained via detail polling\n66\t* \\[P1\\] Polling pauses when the browser tab is not in the foreground (Page Visibility API) and resumes immediately on tab focus\n67\t* \\[P2\\] Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction using `status.message.message_code` where available\n68\t\n69\t### Dependencies\n70\t\n71\t| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n72\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n73\t| eval-hub-ui Module Federation plugin infrastructure | Internal | Exists | Plugin container and BFF proxy path already deployed at port 8543 |\n74\t| odh-dashboard BFF proxy routing | Internal | Exists | /eval-hub/api/\\\\\\* proxy path already configured; transparent field passthrough is the existing pattern |\n75\t| EvalHub Server GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs API | Internal | Exists | List endpoint returns `status.state`, `status.message` for all jobs -- sufficient for top-level state display |\n76\t| EvalHub Server GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id} API | Internal | Exists | Detail endpoint returns `status.benchmarks[]` with per-benchmark status and timestamps -- required for benchmark-level progress |\n77\t| PatternFly status and progress components | External | Exists | PatternFly provides Label, Icon, Alert, and progress components used in this strategy |\n78\t| React Query (TanStack Query) | External | Exists | Already used by autorag and automl packages in the odh-dashboard monorepo |\n79\t\n80\t### Non-Functional Requirements\n81\t\n82\t* **Performance**: Detail polling runs on a 10-second cycle with a maximum of 5 concurrent requests per user session, capped to the jobs visible on the current table page (max 20 rows). The existing list endpoint continues its 30-second polling cycle. Polling pauses when the browser tab is backgrounded (Page Visibility API) and stops for terminal-state jobs. (Staff Engineer / SME Input: poll only visible-page jobs, 5 parallel max, round-robin) (architecture context: eval-hub.md -- EvalHub auth middleware uses 2min authn cache TTL, 5min authz allow cache, 30s deny cache; repeated polls from the same user within the cache window impose minimal auth overhead)\n83\t* **Security**: All requests authenticated via kube-rbac-proxy sidecar (port 8443) with SubjectAccessReview enforcement. Multi-tenant namespace isolation via X-Tenant header in EvalHub Server. No new auth paths introduced. (architecture context: odh-dashboard.md, eval-hub.md)\n84\t* **Backwards Compatibility**: The auto-refresh and progress display are additive. The existing list endpoint polling and table rendering continue to function as-is. Detail polling enriches in-progress rows with additional data -- if the detail endpoint is unavailable, rows degrade gracefully to showing only top-level state from the list endpoint.\n85\t\n86\t### Out-of-Scope\n87\t\n88\t* EvalHub Server API extensions for task-level progress fields (not needed -- strategy uses existing API data)\n89\t* Modification of the list endpoint to include `status.benchmarks[]` (would require Server-side API changes)\n90\t* Failure detail surface, error messages, or execution log access (covered by RHAISTRAT-7670 / RHAIRFE-7237)\n91\t* In-app completion or failure notifications (covered by RHAISTRAT-7671 / RHAIRFE-7238)\n92\t* Server-side push notification infrastructure such as WebSocket or SSE (covered by RHAIRFE-7141)\n93\t* External notification channels (email, Slack, webhook)\n94\t* Execution log access via CLI or SDK (covered by RHAIRFE-7761)\n95\t* Aggregate job monitoring or multi-user evaluation dashboards\n96\t* Kueue queue-specific scheduling state (covered by RHAIRFE-7024)\n97\t* Historical job duration statistics for ETA estimation (future consideration)\n98\t* Replacement of polling with push-based updates via server callbacks (future integration with RHAIRFE-7141)\n99\t* Scalability optimization for high concurrent user counts (deferred per Staff Engineer direction for this release)\n100\t\n101\t### Acceptance Criteria (Proposed -- requires PM/Engineering validation)\n102\t\n103\t* Given an evaluation job is in the running state with 3 of 5 benchmarks completed, when a user views the Evaluations landing page and the detail-polling cycle has completed for that job, then benchmark-level progress is displayed (e.g., \"Benchmarks: 3/5 complete\"), measured by the count matching the number of entries in the `status.benchmarks[]` array with `status: \"completed\"` in the GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id} response.\n104\t\n105\t* Given an evaluation job transitions from running to complete, when the user is on the Evaluations landing page without manually refreshing the browser, then the status update is visible within the list endpoint's polling interval (30 seconds) for the state badge, measured by the job list row updating its state label automatically.\n106\t\n107\t* Given an evaluation job has been running for 4 minutes, when the detail-polling cycle has fetched the job's detail endpoint, then elapsed time is displayed (e.g., \"Elapsed: 4m 12s\"), measured by the elapsed time being the difference between the current time and the earliest per-benchmark `started_at` timestamp in the detail response.\n108\t\n109\t* Given an evaluation job is queued/pending (not yet started), when a user views the Evaluations landing page, then the job row displays a distinct \"Queued\" label and visual treatment that is visually distinguishable from \"Running\" and \"Failed\" states, measured by distinct icon, color, and label text per the PatternFly status pattern.\n110\t\n111\t* Given an evaluation job failed before execution started (e.g., admission error indicated by `status.message.message_code`), when a user views the Evaluations landing page, then the job row displays a distinct visual treatment that differentiates it from a job that ran and then failed, measured by a label reflecting the failure type and an icon/color distinct from the runtime \"Failed\" state.\n112\t\n113\t* Given the EvalHub Server is temporarily unreachable during detail polling, when the eval-hub-ui frontend encounters a 5xx or network error on a detail fetch, then the last successfully fetched benchmark progress for that job remains displayed, a non-blocking inline warning is shown, and the round-robin continues with the next in-progress job while the failed request retries with exponential backoff (up to 5 retries), measured by the UI continuing to render stale detail data for the affected job and the warning Alert component being visible.\n114\t\n115\t* Given the user navigates away from the browser tab, when the tab loses focus, then detail polling pauses and no further requests are made until the tab regains focus, at which point a single polling cycle is triggered immediately, measured by no network requests to the detail endpoint while the tab is backgrounded (verified via browser dev tools Network tab).\n116\t\n117\t### Effort Estimate\n118\t\n119\t**Size: S (Small, 1-2 sprints)**\n120\t\n121\tJustification: The work is frontend-only within the eval-hub-ui plugin -- adding a detail-polling layer with round-robin concurrency control on top of the existing list-endpoint polling, benchmark-level progress display derived from existing API fields, state differentiation using PatternFly components, Page Visibility API integration, and client-side elapsed time computation. No Server-side API changes are required. The BFF proxy path exists and transparently passes response fields. Single team (ODH Dashboard) owns all implementation with UX/Design review for state icon/color specifications. The concurrency control and round-robin logic adds modest complexity compared to a simple polling hook, but the pattern is well-understood and constrained to a single hook.\n122\t\n123\t### Risks\n124\t\n125\t| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n126\t| --- | --- | --- |\n127\t| Benchmark-level progress (X/N complete) is too coarse for jobs with few benchmarks (e.g., 1-benchmark job shows only 0% or 100%) | Users perceive no progress for single-benchmark evaluation runs | Display elapsed time as the primary progress indicator for all jobs; benchmark count is supplementary. For single-benchmark jobs, the running state with elapsed time provides the feedback the RFE requires |\n128\t| `status.message.message_code` values do not consistently distinguish pre-start failures from runtime failures across all error scenarios | UI cannot reliably differentiate \"Failed (not started)\" from \"Failed\" in all cases | Fall back to a single \"Failed\" state with `status.message.message` text displayed as supplementary detail when message_code is ambiguous or absent |\n129\t| Detail polling for up to 20 in-progress jobs on a page creates per-job API calls that may not scale under high concurrent user count | Server performance degradation if many users are viewing pages with many running jobs | Max 5 concurrent requests per session; round-robin ensures fair distribution; polling pauses on background tabs (Page Visibility API); scalability optimization deferred per Staff Engineer direction for this release |\n130\t| React Query retry pattern from autorag has not been tested in the eval-hub-ui Module Federation context with per-job detail fetching | Polling may behave differently due to BFF proxy chain differences or concurrent request management | Validate in development by simulating 5xx errors from the eval-hub-ui BFF on individual detail endpoints; the BFF proxy architecture is identical across autorag/automl/eval-hub (same Go router, same proxy chain) |\n131\t\n132\t### Assumptions\n133\t\n134\t* The EvalHub Server's `status.state` field in the list endpoint response includes values sufficient to distinguish pending, running, completed, and failed states -- confirmed (API docs show `pending`, `completed`; running and failed states are implied by the status model)\n135\t* The `status.benchmarks[]` array in the detail endpoint response is populated during job execution (not only after completion) so that benchmark-level progress can be derived mid-run -- needs validation\n136\t* The `status.message.message_code` field provides distinguishable values for pre-start failures (admission errors, scheduling failures) vs. runtime errors -- needs validation\n137\t* The odh-dashboard BFF proxy transparently passes all response fields from the eval-hub-ui plugin backend without filtering -- confirmed (existing pattern for all Module Federation plugins)\n138\t* PatternFly provides suitable Label, Icon, and Alert components for state differentiation and warning banners -- confirmed\n139\t* The existing list endpoint 30s polling in `useEvaluationJobs` continues to function and can coexist with the new detail-polling layer -- confirmed (React Query supports multiple queries per component)\n140\t\n141\t### Open Questions\n142\t\n143\t| Question | Why It Matters | Owner |\n144\t| --- | --- | --- |\n145\t| Is the `status.benchmarks[]` array populated with per-benchmark status entries while a job is still running, or only after completion? | If only populated on completion, benchmark-level progress (X/N) is unavailable mid-run, and the UI can only show elapsed time + overall state | TrustyAI / EvalHub team |\n146\t| What values does `status.message.message_code` take for pre-start failures (admission error, resource quota, scheduling failure) vs. runtime failures? | Determines whether the UI can reliably distinguish \"Failed (not started)\" from \"Failed (runtime)\" | TrustyAI / EvalHub team |\n147\t\n148\t### Supporting Documentation\n149\t\n150\t* Source RFE: RHAIRFE-7236\n151\t* EvalHub Server API reference: [GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}](https://eval-hub.github.io/eval-hub/index-private.html#tag/Evaluations/operation/get_evaluations_jobs_id)\n152\t* EvalHub Server API reference: [GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs](https://eval-hub.github.io/eval-hub/index-private.html#tag/Evaluations/operation/get_evaluations_jobs)\n153\t* Architecture context: eval-hub.md (EvalHub Server API, auth model, adapter architecture)\n154\t* Architecture context: odh-dashboard.md (Module Federation plugin architecture, BFF proxy, kube-rbac-proxy auth flow)\n155\t* Reference implementation: [autorag usePipelineRunQuery polling pattern](https://github.com/opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard/blob/main/packages/autorag/frontend/src/app/hooks/queries.ts#L266)\n156\t* Reference: eval-hub-ui existing job list implementation (EvaluationsPage.tsx, useEvaluationJobs.ts, k8s.ts)\n157\t* Design doc: \\_To be created -- progress indicator UX specification\\_\n158\t* ADR: \\_To be created if needed\\_\n159\t\n160\t### Prerequisites & Process Gates\n161\t\n162\t| Prerequisite | Status | Notes |\n163\t| --- | --- | --- |\n164\t| Architecture Review | \\_Pending human review\\_ |  |\n165\t| ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding | \\_Pending human review\\_ | eval-hub-ui plugin already onboarded |\n166\t| Licence Validation | \\_Pending human review\\_ |  |\n167\t| Accelerator/Package Support | \\_Pending human review\\_ | N/A -- UI-only change |\n168\t| Documentation Support | \\_Pending human review\\_ |  |\n169\t| UXD Support | \\_Pending human review\\_ | Required for progress indicator pattern, state icon/color specs |\n170\t| Performance Team Support | \\_Pending human review\\_ | Polling load impact assessment |\n171\t\n172\t## Staff Engineer Input\n173\t\n174\tReview the GET api endpoint from evalhub https://eval-hub.github.io/eval-hub/index-private. html#tag/Evaluations/operation/get_evaluations_jobs_id Source this document as supporting documentation and use it to identify what data is actually available in the response for a running job. Assume we do not have any more data available to us other than what is already available from this api. Adjust the plan accordingly.\n175\t\n176\tThe detail endpoint (GET /evaluations/jobs/{id}) returns several fields that the list endpoint (GET /evaluations/jobs) omits. Since the jobs table is populated with a lost\n177\t\n178\t| Field | List | Detail |\n179\t| --- | --- | --- |\n180\t| `description` | missing | present |\n181\t| `tags` | missing | present |\n182\t| `status.benchmarks[]` (per-benchmark status with `started_at`, `completed_at`) | missing | present |\n183\t| `results.benchmarks[].mlflow_run_id` | missing | present |\n184\t| `results.benchmarks[].logs_path` | missing | present |\n185\t| `benchmarks[].primary_score` (config-level metric, `lower_is_better`) | missing | present |\n186\t| `benchmarks[].pass_criteria` (per-benchmark threshold) | missing | present |\n187\t| `benchmarks[].parameters` | missing | present |\n188\t\n189\tIn short, the list endpoint returns a trimmed-down job object — it has the core identity (resource, name, model), top-level status/results, and benchmark config IDs, but drops the detailed per-benchmark runtime status, artifact references (mlflow_run_id, logs_path), full benchmark configuration (parameters, primary_score, per-benchmark pass_criteria), and metadata fields (description, tags).\n190\t\n191\tThe evaluations page uses the list endpoint only. The flow is:\n192\t\n193\tEvaluationsPage.tsx calls useEvaluationJobs() useEvaluationJobs.ts wraps getEvaluationJobs() with useFetchState and 30s polling k8s.ts:84-116 makes a single GET /eval-hub/api/v1/evaluations/jobs call and returns the items array It does not call individual job endpoints (/jobs/{id}) for the list page. That single-job endpoint is used separately by useEvaluationJob on the EvaluationResultsPage.\n194\t\n195\tThis means the list page won't have access to detail-only fields like description, tags, status.benchmarks\\[\\], mlflow_run_id, logs_path, or per-benchmark parameters/pass_criteria. To represent status in the list view, we will need to build a list of the jobs on the current displayed page which are currently in progress and poll those with the detail endpoint. Only 5 parallel fetches should happen at one time, in a round robin. Only the jobs currently in view in the evaluations list table should be polled. The jobs which are not in view should not be polled unless the page is turned in the table and they are now in view. The table is client-side paginated showing 20 jobs per page by default.\n196\t\n197\tPer-page options: 5, 10, or 20 (defined in tablePaginationConstants.ts) Pagination logic: All jobs are fetched in a single API call with no limit/offset, then filtered → sorted → sliced in memory at EvaluationsTable.tsx:168-171 Page resets to 1 when filters change.\n198\t\n199\tFor the polling case where there are errors, replicate the pattern used in autorag and automl packages where polling is retried with exponential backoff. A non blocking warning is shown to the user that the ui is not updating but polling should still be attempted with the last successful status still being displayed. See https://github.com/opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard/blob/main/packages/autorag/frontend/src/app/hooks/queries.ts#L266\n200\t\n201\tAddressing open questions:\n202\t\n203\t> What is the acceptable latency window for state transition visibility -- is 10 seconds sufficient?\n204\t\n205\t10 seconds is sufficient\n206\t\n207\t> Should polling pause when the browser tab is not in the foreground (Page Visibility API)?\n208\t\n209\tYes to save resources\n210\t\n211\t> No performance target specified in RFE or architecture context for maximum concurrent polling users -- needs PM/Engineering input\n212\t\n213\tWe should poll only the jobs currently in view.\n214\t\n215\t> No performance target specified in RFE or architecture context for maximum concurrent polling users -- needs PM/Engineering input\n216\t\n217\tFor mvp of this release we will poll 5 jobs in parallel up to the total of 20 displayed on the page the user is currently viewing. It should only have 5 active calls at once, and switch to the next call as one completes in a round robin. When all calls complete, then timer should kick off for the next poll. We will not consider scalability for this release, all user polling behaviour is the same.\n218\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5132abc16296698","agent_id":"af25faade99ebe470","session_id":"f780de70-496a-445e-b2ff-c0c509886095","uuid":"aca8fded-62b4-4095-80fd-09191673d73e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:46:44.900Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b06588fa89c7c2e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8005 decomposition"}
