{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-afc593286abcbe93","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8005 — EvalHub UI: Real-Time Evaluation Job Progress and State Visibility\n10\t\n11\t## Triage Outcome\n12\t\n13\tThe strategy is S-sized, single-active-component (eval-hub-ui plugin), and single-team (ODH Dashboard), but the below-threshold path does **not** apply: the strategy's Open Questions table contains two genuine unknowns explicitly marked \"needs validation\" in the Assumptions section — (1) whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated in the detail endpoint response while a job is still running, and (2) whether `status.message.message_code` reliably distinguishes pre-start failures from runtime failures. Per Step 6.5, strategy-flagged open questions are genuine unknowns and must not be downgraded to implementation details. The answer to (1) determines whether the detail-polling layer, benchmark-level progress display, and `started_at`-based elapsed time are implementable as specified (a change to what the implementation epic does); the answer to (2) determines whether the P2 pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction is implementable. Full decomposition proceeds.\n14\t\n15\t## Staff Engineer Input (Step 1.5)\n16\t\n17\tThe Staff Engineer Input section is non-empty and was parsed first; it takes precedence. It is consistent with the AI-generated Technical Approach (the strategy was already adjusted to it), so no divergences required override logging. Directions incorporated from Staff Engineer Input:\n18\t\n19\t- No data beyond the two existing API endpoints (`GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs`, `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}`) is assumed; the plan is built on what those endpoints actually return.\n20\t- Two-layer polling: existing 30s list polling for top-level state; new 10s detail-polling layer for in-progress jobs on the currently displayed table page only (client-side pagination, default 20 rows, options 5/10/20).\n21\t- Max 5 concurrent detail requests, round-robin (next job fetched as one completes); a 10s timer starts when all detail fetches for the current page complete.\n22\t- Retry-on-error replicates the autorag `usePipelineRunQuery` React Query pattern (placeholderData preserving last success, exponential backoff with jitter, 4xx non-retryable, 5xx/network retried); non-blocking warning while stale data is shown.\n23\t- Page Visibility API pause/resume confirmed (\"yes, to save resources\"); 10-second latency window confirmed sufficient.\n24\t- Scalability for high concurrent user counts is explicitly deferred for this release.\n25\t\n26\tThe Staff Engineer Input also resolved the strategy's earlier latency/concurrency questions (the three quoted Q&A blocks); they are no longer unknowns. The two API-data questions above remain open and drive the Investigation epic.\n27\t\n28\t## Epic List\n29\t\n30\t| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n31\t|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 | Validate mid-run availability of benchmark progress and failure-code data in the EvalHub Server job status API | Investigation | AI Evaluations | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E002 | Progress indicator and state differentiation UX specification for the EvalHub Evaluations page | Implementation | UXD | UX/Design | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E003 | EvalHub UI two-layer polling, progress display, and state differentiation | Implementation | AI Core Dashboard | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n35\t\n36\t## Dependency DAG\n37\t\n38\t```mermaid\n39\tgraph TD\n40\t  RHAISTRAT8005E001[\"E001: Validate EvalHub Server mid-run status data (Investigation)\"]\n41\t  RHAISTRAT8005E002[\"E002: Progress indicator / state UX specification (Implementation)\"]\n42\t  RHAISTRAT8005E003[\"E003: EvalHub UI polling, progress display, state differentiation (Implementation)\"]\n43\t  RHAISTRAT8005E001 --> RHAISTRAT8005E003\n44\t  RHAISTRAT8005E002 --> RHAISTRAT8005E003\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\t## DAG Justification\n48\t\n49\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n50\t|------|------|-----------|\n51\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementations) | The validation answers whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated mid-run and whether `message_code` distinguishes failure classes. The answers change what the implementation epic builds (benchmark-count progress vs. elapsed-time-only; pre-start failure distinction vs. single Failed state). True gate recorded on E003 as `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n52\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (producer of an artifact the consumer builds against) | The implementation consumes the UX specification (state icon/color specs, progress indicator pattern) as its design contract; the strategy marks UXD Support as required for exactly these deliverables. |\n53\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 14 (independent inputs) | The UX specification is design authoring that does not need the API validation result first: the strategy already directs elapsed-time-primary presentation with benchmark count supplementary, so the spec is written to cover both possible validation outcomes. They start in parallel. |\n54\t\n55\t**Why no conditional branch epics:** both validation questions have bounded (≤2) outcomes, but no outcome changes downstream *structure* — the same single implementation epic exists either way, with scope adjusted (benchmark-count display dropped, elapsed-time fallback used; single Failed state fallback). This is exactly the `rewrite` gate captured in E003's `gate_failure_impact`, so no `-BRANCH-` files are warranted.\n56\t\n57\t**Why one implementation epic (no priority split):** Step 4's priority-split test fails for every P1/P2 HLR — benchmark progress, elapsed time, Page Visibility pause, and the pre-start failure distinction all share the same UI surface (the job table row), the same data structures (the detail-polling hook and job status state), and the same API endpoints as the P0 state-differentiation and auto-refresh work. They are tightly coupled to the same implementation surface and stay bundled.\n58\t\n59\t**Why no separate epic for the odh-dashboard BFF:** the BFF is a passive component (\"verify passthrough — no code change expected\") owned by the same team (ODH Dashboard) as the active component, so per Step 2 its validation is folded in as an acceptance criterion on the implementation epic.\n60\t\n61\t**Why no docs-authoring epic:** the strategy requires no substantive documentation content (new tutorials, doc topics, sample notebooks); Documentation Support is only a pending-review line item. Incidental doc touch-ups fall out of the code change and stay in the implementation epic's scope.\n62\t\n63\t**Why no tiered delivery:** no external dependency has uncertain timing — PatternFly and React Query both exist and are already in use (autorag/automl packages); all internal dependencies (plugin infrastructure, BFF proxy path, both EvalHub Server endpoints) are marked \"Exists\".\n64\t\n65\t## Component Mappings\n66\t\n67\tCanonical names taken from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n68\t\n69\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n70\t|----|----|----|\n71\t| eval-hub-ui (odh-dashboard plugin) | AI Core Dashboard | No canonical \"EvalHub UI\" component exists; the plugin lives in the odh-dashboard repository (Module Federation module `packages/eval-hub`) and is owned by the ODH Dashboard team, so the closest parent-level component is used. |\n72\t| odh-dashboard (BFF) | AI Core Dashboard | Passive component (no code change expected); validation folded as an AC into E003. |\n73\t| EvalHub Server (source of the open questions) | AI Evaluations | Closest canonical match for the eval-hub service (architecture context file `eval-hub.md` exists — component is in platform). |\n74\t| Dashboard design system (UX spec) | UXD | Canonical component for UX design work; the epic produces design specifications rather than code. |\n75\t\n76\tBoth eval-hub and odh-dashboard have architecture-context files, so no new-component onboarding chain (Rule 20) applies. No repo/konflux/license onboarding is needed — the plugin is already onboarded per the strategy's prerequisites.\n77\t\n78\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n79\t\n80\t| HLR | Priority | Epics | Notes |\n81\t|-----|----------|-------|-------|\n82\t| HLR-1: Auto-refresh with list + detail polling for state transitions | P0 | E003 | Detail-polling portion gated by E001 |\n83\t| HLR-2: Visual distinction of queued/running/failed/completed states | P0 | E002 (specification), E003 (implementation) | |\n84\t| HLR-3: Non-blocking error handling during detail polling | P0 | E003 | |\n85\t| HLR-4: Benchmark-level progress display with max-5 round-robin fetch | P1 | E003 | Gated by E001 (benchmarks[] mid-run availability) |\n86\t| HLR-5: Elapsed time display from per-benchmark started_at | P1 | E003 | started_at source gated by E001; resource.created_at fallback exists |\n87\t| HLR-6: Page Visibility API pause/resume | P1 | E003 | |\n88\t| HLR-7: Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction via message_code | P2 | E003 | Gated by E001 (message_code semantics) |\n89\t\n90\tAll seven HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. Priority inheritance: E001 and E002 are prerequisites of E003, which carries P0 HLRs, so both inherit P0. An epic gating P0 work is implicitly P0.\n91\t\n92\t## Health Warnings\n93\t\n94\t- **Contingent P0 scope (non-blocking):** P0 HLR-1 embeds the detail-polling layer whose mid-run value depends on E001's outcome. If `status.benchmarks[]` is only populated after completion, P0 scope narrows to list-driven state transitions plus elapsed time from `resource.created_at`, and the strategy's benchmark-progress acceptance 