{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bef624fd56dd2dea","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8005-E001\n3\ttitle: Validate mid-run availability of benchmark progress and failure-code data in\n4\t  the EvalHub Server job status API\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8005\n6\tcomponent: AI Evaluations\n7\tteam: TrustyAI / EvalHub\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 1\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tdependencies: []\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Medium\n20\tai_implementability_score: 1\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Validate mid-run availability of benchmark progress and failure-code data in the EvalHub Server job status API\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation resolves the two open questions from the strategy that gate the EvalHub UI progress and state-visibility features. Both concern what the EvalHub Server actually returns while an evaluation job is still running:\n31\t\n32\t1. Is `status.benchmarks[]` in the `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}` detail response populated with per-benchmark status entries (per-benchmark `status`, `started_at`, `completed_at`) **while the job is running**, or only after completion? If only on completion, benchmark-level progress (\"Benchmarks: X/N complete\") and `started_at`-based elapsed time are unavailable mid-run, and the UI can only show top-level state plus elapsed time derived from `resource.created_at`.\n33\t2. What values does `status.message.message_code` take for pre-start failures (admission errors, resource quota, scheduling failures) versus runtime failures? Are the two classes reliably distinguishable, or is a fallback to a single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text required?\n34\t\n35\tThe EvalHub Server is an open Go repository (architecture context: `eval-hub.md`), so both questions are answerable primarily by reading the server source code that constructs job status responses and emits error messages, supplemented by the published API reference. The server supports a local/development mode (identity-header enforcement bypassed, SQLite storage backend), though empirically confirming mid-run population for a live evaluation job would require a cluster, since evaluation workloads are dispatched as Kubernetes batch Jobs.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\t- Trace the EvalHub Server code paths that build the job status object for the detail endpoint: where `status.benchmarks[]` entries are created and updated relative to the job lifecycle (job admission, per-benchmark start, per-benchmark completion, job completion), and which fields each entry carries at each point.\n40\t- Trace the error-reporting paths that populate `status.message` and `status.message.message_code`: enumerate the codes produced by admission/scheduling/quota failures versus runtime execution failures, and note any scenarios where the code is absent or ambiguous.\n41\t- Cross-check findings against the published API reference for `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}` and `GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs`.\n42\t- Produce a findings report with a clear pass/fail conclusion per question, source citations, and — for each unfavorable outcome — confirmation that the strategy's fallback UI behavior is feasible against the actual API data (elapsed time from `resource.created_at`; single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text as supplementary detail).\n43\t\n44\t## Acceptance Criteria\n45\t\n46\t- A concrete, source-cited answer to whether `status.benchmarks[]` entries (with per-benchmark status, `started_at`, `completed_at`) are present in detail responses while a job is running, including the lifecycle point at which each entry appears.\n47\t- An enumerated list of `status.message.message_code` values produced by pre-start failure paths (admission, quota, scheduling) versus runtime failure paths, with a conclusion on whether the two classes are reliably distinguishable across error scenarios.\n48\t- For each unfavorable outcome, a confirmed statement of the fallback UI behavior and the exact fields it can rely on.\n49\t\n50\t## HLR Traceability\n51\t\n52\tThis investigation does not implement any HLR directly; it gates the implementation of:\n53\t\n54\t- HLR-1 (P0) — the detail-polling portion of the auto-refresh mechanism\n55\t- HLR-4 (P1) — benchmark-level progress display\n56\t- HLR-5 (P1) — elapsed time display from per-benchmark `started_at` (fallback: `resource.created_at`)\n57\t- HLR-7 (P2) — pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction via `message_code`\n58\t\n59\t## Open Questions Resolved\n60\t\n61\t- \"Is `status.benchmarks[]` populated with per-benchmark status entries while a job is still running, or only after completion?\" (strategy Open Questions table, owner: TrustyAI / EvalHub team)\n62\t- \"What values does `status.message.message_code` take for pre-start failures vs. runtime failures?\" (strategy Open Questions table, owner: TrustyAI / EvalHub team)\n63\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa6efacfd4c31f49","agent_id":"a1366b6e01b1563af","session_id":"f780de70-496a-445e-b2ff-c0c509886095","uuid":"c08bf783-4c18-4d36-829b-b8ed8bf325bd","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:51:24.058Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bef624fd56dd2dea","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8005 decomposition"}
